<h2>The variety question that shapes every basmati order</h2>
<p>Before processing type, before moisture spec, before packing format — the first real decision on a basmati order is variety. 1121 and Pusa (1509, 1401) account for the large majority of India's basmati export volume, and they're different enough commercially that "basmati rice" alone isn't a usable specification.</p>

<h2>1121: the benchmark for grain length</h2>
<p>1121 basmati averages 8.30-8.40mm grain length before cooking — the longest of any commercially traded basmati variety — and is prized above all for its post-cooking elongation, extending further without breaking than competing varieties. This makes it the dominant choice for Middle East demand, particularly for biryani and pulao-style dishes where visual grain length and texture are central to the dish's presentation and appeal. That performance commands a genuine price premium over other basmati types.</p>

<h2>Pusa: strong quality at better value</h2>
<p>Pusa varieties, principally 1509 and 1401, run somewhat shorter at 7.80-8.20mm average grain length. They still deliver good basmati characteristics — aroma, texture, reasonable elongation — at a lower price point than 1121, making them the practical choice for volume-focused buyers where cost efficiency matters more than maximum grain length. A meaningful share of global basmati demand is served well by Pusa without needing 1121's premium characteristics.</p>

<h2>Aging: a separate lever, applicable to both</h2>
<p>Aging — holding milled rice for 12 or 24 months under controlled conditions before shipment — improves cooking performance in both varieties, reducing water absorption and stickiness while improving elongation further. Aged stock commands its own premium independent of variety, and buyers focused on the best possible cooking result often specify both variety (1121) and aging (24 months) together for maximum quality, accepting the combined price premium that results.</p>

<h2>Processing type is a third, independent variable</h2>
<p>Raw, steam, sella (parboiled) and golden sella processing apply to both 1121 and Pusa, and affect texture, breakage rate and cooking behaviour independently of variety choice. A poorly processed 1121 can underperform a well-processed Pusa on final cooked quality — variety alone doesn't determine the outcome, which is why our <a href="/products/grains-rice/basmati-rice/">basmati rice specification framework</a> treats variety, processing, grain length, moisture, broken percentage, purity and aging as seven separate parameters to agree, not one blended "quality" judgment.</p>

<h2>Making the choice</h2>
<p>Ask what your end customer actually values. Premium retail or food-service positioning built around maximum grain length and elongation points toward 1121. Volume-focused business where cost efficiency at good quality is the priority points toward Pusa. Many of our buyers order both, allocating each to the customer segment it fits best.</p>
